<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GG">Remark <span data-tag="06GG">9.3.7.3</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="06GF" href="/tag/06GF">9.3.7.2</a>, we can identify $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ with the full subcategory of $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \lambda } )$ spanned by the $\kappa $-flat functors (Theorem <a data-tag="065X" href="/tag/065X">9.3.4.14</a>). Consequently, to show that $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ has a final object, it suffices to show that the constant functor $\mathscr {F}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \{  \Delta ^0 \}  \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ is $\kappa $-flat. This is a reformulation of the hypothesis that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-filtered, since $\mathscr {F}$ is the contravariant transport representation for the right fibration $\operatorname{id}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
